.3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8503a82e-cf30-5876-b2c2-50de2ef2f695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410430 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{9}=3,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.3.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.3 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{3}{0.3} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >10 \\\\ n & >{10}^{2} \\\\ n & >100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=101.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411687 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411687 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411687 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08bfd88b-acbe-5297-8f06-a9f5f59416bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411689 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}e^{-2(t-2)}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411689 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411689 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f87f2fe-f5bd-58d0-8229-020d3f04a17d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411690 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186411690 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411690 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "118a9ee1-aa44-5777-9118-5227ee29fe39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411692 #edb/ref 1080 "-3\\sin(3t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411692 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411692 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1283b6db-b266-5d77-a129-b29326dc0d71" 